<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Japanese Village (Ayutthaya)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ban Yipun, Muban Yipun, Ayutaya Nihonjin-machi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Japanese Village, Ban Yipun in Thai, was a Japanese enclave just outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>, the capital of the Ayutthaya Kingdom, that flourished from the 17th century to the mid-18th century. Its people, perhaps 1,000 to 1,500, were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, traders and Christians fleeing persecution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, along with Thai and Chinese slaves. Ayutthaya hired Japanese soldiers against the Burmese, and their leaders could hold the Phraya rank, third highest in the Siamese hierarchy, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamada-nagamasa">Yamada Nagamasa</a> did. In 1630, after Nagamasa's assassination, the settlement was burned and its residents killed; refounded in 1633, it gradually merged into the Thai population.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/japanese-village-ayutthaya.jpg" alt="Japanese Village (Ayutthaya)" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: กสิณธร ราชโอรส · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:หมู่บ้านญี่ปุ่น_อ.พระนครศรีอยุธยา_จ.พระนครศรีอยุธยา_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
